WEBVTT

00:02.469 --> 00:04.871
And this is Joe Bevington.

00:04.871 --> 00:09.709
We're sitting in Cocoa Beach Cafe,
and this is in Cornwall, UK.

00:09.809 --> 00:13.747
I am CEO of Cook in Cornwall, CIC.

00:13.813 --> 00:19.352
And predominantly our work is to support
new migrants to Cornwall

00:19.419 --> 00:23.523
to promote blackness in Cornwall

00:23.590 --> 00:27.794
as a way of feed and acceptance.

00:27.894 --> 00:32.365
I was born in the early sixties
in West Bromwich, England,

00:32.432 --> 00:35.068
but I grew up in the Caribbean.

00:35.068 --> 00:40.273
And then when I was 15, I came back
to live in England with my mum.

00:40.373 --> 00:42.042
I'm a country girl at heart.

00:42.042 --> 00:44.677
Even when I lived in Jamaica,
I lived in Kingston

00:44.677 --> 00:48.748
with every single opportunity
and went away to the countryside

00:48.815 --> 00:52.419
because I think the less urban areas

00:52.419 --> 00:57.257
afford a sense of freedom
and the opportunity to explore.

00:57.290 --> 01:00.293
In a sense that is not contrived.

01:00.293 --> 01:03.096
And that's one of the things that I wanted
for my child.

01:03.096 --> 01:06.032
And I knew that

01:06.099 --> 01:06.499
no matter

01:06.499 --> 01:09.502
where we live, she was going to have

01:09.536 --> 01:10.937
challenges.

01:10.937 --> 01:13.173
So why not

01:13.173 --> 01:17.811
support by letting her
have the freedom to space

01:17.911 --> 01:21.948
and not the competition
against her in an urban area?

01:21.948 --> 01:24.951
Because that can be
so much more difficult.

01:24.951 --> 01:29.656
There was a time when you would count
how many persons of color

01:29.656 --> 01:34.227
you saw on the cover on High Street,
and it was a couple of Christmases ago.

01:34.461 --> 01:39.799
I stood and I was counting at
I counted 21 adults

01:39.899 --> 01:44.070
within one hour and I was like, ooh!

01:44.137 --> 01:45.705
And I stopped, counted.

01:45.705 --> 01:47.674
I realized that

01:47.674 --> 01:50.610
people were mobilized into
to come into Cornwall.

01:50.610 --> 01:53.012
They were living here now.

01:53.012 --> 01:54.981
It's such a beautiful thing to see.

01:54.981 --> 01:58.685
I'm all about feeding people's soul
and their bodies and things like that.

01:58.918 --> 02:02.455
And removing the the stigma.

02:02.522 --> 02:05.558
My chilies that I use here,
my peppers and chilies that I use that

02:05.592 --> 02:07.660
it's grown locally,

02:07.727 --> 02:08.161
right by.

02:08.161 --> 02:11.097
CORNISH People were chili enthusiasts.

02:11.097 --> 02:13.500
And in this little café
that we've got here,

02:13.500 --> 02:16.503
one of the joys is we can have 50 people
here.

02:16.603 --> 02:19.139
We've got eight different nationalities,

02:19.139 --> 02:22.842
just like joy to
where the world should be.

02:22.942 --> 02:26.579
But we still retain our willingness,
and that is the thing.

02:26.613 --> 02:30.183
So we're trying to engage
the younger generation

02:30.183 --> 02:34.020
so that we can have new cultural
ambassadors, that they can be proud of

02:34.020 --> 02:39.025
being in their skin and be part
of the new Cornwall, as I like to call it.

02:39.092 --> 02:43.930
Traditionally,
black people have come to do the work

02:44.030 --> 02:49.836
that no one else wanted to do
or to come and

02:49.903 --> 02:52.672
support systems that are failing.

02:52.672 --> 02:58.511
But I think people are yearning
for downtime

02:58.578 --> 03:03.883
to get in connection with the soil again,
because they don't see that as work.

03:03.917 --> 03:05.985
They see that as therapy.

03:05.985 --> 03:08.821
But they've got the beach
and the considerato in the Atlantic,

03:08.821 --> 03:10.056
in the CORNISH sea.

03:10.056 --> 03:12.392
We've got the countryside,
we've got walks,

03:12.392 --> 03:15.395
we have places that we can sit
and be safe.

03:15.461 --> 03:18.231
What makes me smile is
see those who were here

03:18.231 --> 03:22.068
from three years ago
now smiling, living in Cornwall.

03:22.068 --> 03:25.872
I don't think I could go anywhere
else in the UK, my home.

03:25.939 --> 03:27.240
I like

03:27.240 --> 03:30.777
people to be celebrated for themselves
first

03:30.843 --> 03:34.781
and the community that they're from after.

03:34.881 --> 03:38.451
And Cornwall is a place as of for the fact

03:38.551 --> 03:42.021
I celebrate my blackness, I celebrate
the fact that I'm neurodivergent

03:42.255 --> 03:46.125
celebrate the fact I'm a female
that celebrate the fact that I'm a mother.

03:46.226 --> 03:49.562
There's so many intersectionality
that makes up

03:49.562 --> 03:53.666
who I am and what Kirkby stands for.

03:53.733 --> 03:56.736
I just think that if
if our black British people

03:56.803 --> 04:00.673
look at themselves, they'll see that
and they see how just wonderful

04:00.673 --> 04:03.643
and the sound that we can give,
how much we have to give,

04:03.643 --> 04:06.779
we should really take
that opportunity to give.
